const e="solo-ensemble-music-festival",c=2026,t="Read the whole 2026-2027 WSMA Festival Rules and Information (the current edition; the template cites 2024-2025, so the newer one is used), the 2026-2027 Adjudicator Handbook (rating bands, Regular vs Special Ensemble rules) and the 2026-2027 Dues and Fees schedule. Placed: purpose, event types and classes, ratings, eligibility (member and non-member students), registration deadlines, late and refund rules, transfers, permissions, alternate editions, event transfers, original compositions, district and state requirements, state qualification, entry limits, performance requirements for solos and ensembles, special ensemble time limits and conducting rules, Class A/B/C rating bands, Exemplary Performance Recognition, conduct and recording rules, fees. Edition facts are Variables: district and state dates and deadlines, fees and late fees, non-member fee, membership dues, WSMA phone and address, links. Differences from 2024-2025: a rule violation now gives No Rating (NR) instead of a critique only; handbell choir is 16 minutes; a solo may be entered once per school year; music theater can also be reached with a Class A duet and duet event 1382 exists; Class A vocal introductions must name the language; pre-recorded accompaniment is now allowed for concert festivals; an Exemplary Performance Recognition Project and a State rating table (I to IV) are printed. The documents do NOT print: district festival dates or sites (set by each district), a state site address, a contact person or email, any prize other than Exemplary Performance certificates, or rubric criteria weights. The change-list document linked as 2026-2027 changes is headed 2025-2026 and was used only to confirm earlier changes. Not carried because they belong to other classes: Concert Festivals, Clinic Experience, Class M, State Honors, State Marching Band and Student Composition Project (named in one line under Page key other). The Festival Music List (event headers) and the rubric forms are in a password-protected WSMA portal and were not read; the music list is the source of per-event rules, so those rules are not captured. The school membership dues and the festival managers' own duties (subsidy, adjudicator assignment) are printed in the sources and kept short. No bank or account details are printed. Wording that differs from the template: the template names its groups by years of experience (Cls C 1-2y exp, Cls B 3-4y exp, Cls A advanced); the rules use Class C, B and A.",s=JSON.parse(`{"Title":"Rules & Criteria","Subtitle":"WSMA Solo & Ensemble Festival (2026-2027 rules). Read these rules before you register.","NoticeTitle":"Please Read Before Registering","Notice":"Teachers register their students through their WSMA member school, online, at least four weeks before the district festival. Entries are placed by years of musical experience (Class A, B or C), not by age or grade. Every entry is adjudicated and receives a Rating, not a place.","Notes":["Bring a publisher-printed original (or legal electronically printed) copy of your music for the adjudicator, labelled with the school name and class.","Every performance starts with a short introduction by a student. The introduction does not count in the rating.","A second performance of the same event is not allowed.","Contact WSMA with any question: {{WsmaOffice}}."],"Sections":[{"Key":"general","Title":"About the Festival","Items":["The Wisconsin School Music Association (WSMA) Solo & Ensemble Festival is held at district level and then at state level. Every performance is adjudicated.","The purpose of WSMA festivals is to improve students' music performance through assessment, to increase their understanding of music literature and music concepts, to motivate them to continue the study of music, to establish standards of excellence in music performance, and to help them see how music experiences relate to other life experiences.","The Festival has three event types: Solo events (vocal, string, woodwind, brass, percussion, piano, guitar and handbell), Regular Ensemble events (the same instrument groups) and Special Ensemble events (miscellaneous ensemble, jazz ensemble, jazz combo, madrigal, vocal jazz, show choir, contemporary a cappella, ethnic heritage ensemble, string, woodwind and brass choir or ensemble, percussion ensemble, guitar ensemble and handbell choir).","Each event is entered in one of three levels of class: A, B or C.","WSMA Festival rules and information govern all WSMA-sanctioned festivals and are written for member schools. A school may add its own rules as an extension of these rules.","Rules for one event are in the event header in the WSMA Festival Music List. If the printed Festival Music List and the online version differ, the online version applies.","WSMA assigns all adjudicators, collects fees, applies the rules, gives a subsidy and offers awards to buy for district festivals.","WSMA sends a subsidy, based on registration fees as approved by the WSMA board, to the district festival manager after WSMA receives all payments from the schools in that district festival.","A district festival manager can add income to pay festival costs, for example a surcharge on individual or class fees or concessions. This extra income is at the discretion of the district.","Each soloist or ensemble performs one selection chosen from the current WSMA Festival Music List. If a selection has several movements, perform two contrasting movements unless the event says otherwise.","Perform all repeats. If a selection is longer than three quarters of the time slot, cut it to fit the time. For example, in an 8-minute slot, cuts are allowed if the full performance is longer than 6 minutes. Cut repeats and repetitive sections first, keep movements or sections in contrasting styles, and clearly mark every cut in the music given to the adjudicator.","Teachers may adapt music for students identified with special needs (for example 504 or IEP). All adapted music and requested accommodations must be sent to WSMA with the Permissions Request Form at {{PermissionsLink}}.","The adjudicator must receive a publisher-printed original of the matching, correct edition of the music. Legal electronically printed music is also allowed. Without it the performance is for critique only (no rating).","Label every score with the school name and class entered, and the soloist's name for a solo. Numbering the measures is highly recommended but not required.","Music saved on an electronic device (for example an iPad) is not allowed for the adjudicator.","Follow federal copyright law for all music. Legally purchased e-print music must show on each page, or on a cover page, that it was purchased by (name) through (music publisher or website). If the publisher gives no statement or cover page, attach proof of purchase.","Public domain music (for example IMSLP) must be sent through permissions as an alternate edition.","Permanently Out of Print (POP) music needs the original legally purchased music, or a letter from the publisher that allows photocopied parts or score.","Each adjudicator can choose a limited number of Exemplary Performances from the WSMA State Solo & Ensemble Festival. See Prizes and Recognition.","Accompanists: the number of events one accompanist can play at a district festival is at the discretion of the festival manager, who may limit events or minutes per accompanist for scheduling and will contact schools or accompanists if there is a scheduling problem.","At a state festival, the number of events per accompanist is at the discretion of WSMA. WSMA recommends that one accompanist not play more than 20 accompaniments in one State Festival weekend. WSMA will contact schools or accompanists if there is a scheduling problem."]},{"Key":"eligibility","Title":"Eligibility","Items":["The school must be a current member of WSMA.","Students must be in grades 6 through 12.","Students must be enrolled in, and regularly attend rehearsals of, a concert group (band, choir or orchestra) at their WSMA member school.","The school decides exceptions to the rehearsal requirement. For example, a schedule conflict with an academic class needed to graduate, or the school offering no suitable group. The building principal or their designee decides eligibility in these cases.","WSMA recommends that these students join other school music activities: an independent music class, a school music ensemble that rehearses outside normal school hours, accompanying another student, or acting as a coach (tutor or peer coach) for other music students.","A school may add its own eligibility policies and expectations for its students.","Home-schooled students and students from a non-member school can enter the District and State Solo & Ensemble Festivals if they pay WSMA an annual enrollment or membership fee of {{NonMemberFee}}.","These students also pay the Solo & Ensemble registration fees in the WSMA Schedule of Dues and Fees.","An ensemble can only include students who attend a WSMA member school or are registered as non-member participants.","Parents or guardians of non-member students should contact WSMA for more information: {{WsmaOffice}}.","Entries are placed in a class by the school's teacher, based on approximate years of musical experience: Class C is one or two years, Class B is three or four years, and Class A is advanced high school musical experience.","The teacher decides the events and the class level. It is strongly recommended that grade 6 students start the Solo & Ensemble experience in Class C.","Ratings are based only on the performance against the rubric. Adjudicators never ask performers their age or grade level.","To perform at a State Solo & Ensemble Festival, a student must receive a starred (*) I rating in a Class A event at a sanctioned WSMA District Solo & Ensemble Festival in the same school year.","Students in a Regular or Special Ensemble may come from more than one school. For this, a co-op ensemble form must be sent to WSMA. All students must attend WSMA member schools or belong to a family enrolled in WSMA, and the member school teachers of all the students must approve forming the ensemble."]},{"Key":"registration","Title":"How to Register","Items":["Registration is made online by the teacher through the WSMA online festival registration system, with the Solo & Ensemble fees. See {{OnlineRegistration}}.","District: register every event online at least four weeks before the district festival. {{DistrictRegistrationDeadline}}","State: register every event online at least 15 business days before the assigned State Solo & Ensemble Festival. {{StateRegistrationDeadline}}","District festival managers can refuse late entries. If a late entry is accepted, an extra cost per entry applies: {{LateRegistrationFee}}.","WSMA can refuse late state entries. If a late state entry is accepted, an extra fee per entry is charged. WSMA can waive the late fee if there are extenuating circumstances.","No refund is given if an entry is withdrawn within four weeks of a district festival, or within 15 business days of the State Festival.","Moving a student to a different district festival needs permission from the festival manager at the other festival site. The first registration fees cannot be moved. They must be paid again if the transfer request comes after the registration deadline of the first festival or after that festival is over. Use the student transfer request form in the online festival registration system.","A student transfer request for the State Festival is sent to the Program Director for approval. A request approved after November 15 of the school year is charged an extra fee per entry ({{StateTransferFee}}). Requests are decided case by case.","A request to move all of a school's state festival entries to another state festival site must reach WSMA by {{StateTransferDeadline}}. WSMA decides alone whether to grant it and may limit the sites by space.","State transfer requests are usually not granted for a school that schedules another music event or tour on the same day as its assigned state festival. Each request is reviewed case by case.","Time requests for the State Festival are made online. They are honored case by case. Contact WSMA for more information.","Music not on the current Festival Music List: where the event header allows permission, submit a Permission Request online at {{PermissionsLink}} at least six weeks before the district festival. If a different selection will be played at state, the request must be received at least three weeks before the state festival.","A permission approval letter is sent to the teacher. Attach it to the adjudicator's music. The performer gets a rating only if the letter is attached.","If the district selection needed a permission approval, the same permission approval letter is needed for the State Festival.","Alternate editions: a different, comparable edition of any selection on the current Festival Music List (even events marked No Permission) can be requested online at {{PermissionsLink}}. The request must reach WSMA at least six weeks before the festival, and the approval letter must be attached to the adjudicator's music for a rating.","Event transfers: music from the current Festival Music List can be used in events marked Transfer Allowed. Transferred titles need no permission. The transfer is done during event registration. Music from any of the 6000 events cannot be moved to other event numbers.","Original compositions must be sent to WSMA for permission. Original compositions by currently enrolled eligible students can be submitted in all WSMA festival events, including those marked No Permission or Transfer. Other original compositions or arrangements can be submitted only in events marked Permission Allowed.","A school can ask to change its assigned district festival site before {{DistrictSiteChangeDeadline}}. The request needs approval from WSMA, the school principal, the district festival manager of the site now assigned and the district festival manager of the requested site."]},{"Key":"levels","Title":"District and State Festivals","Items":["The Solo & Ensemble Festival has two levels: District and State.","At least three WSMA member schools are needed to form a District Solo & Ensemble Festival.","The participating schools set the district festival dates and formats. {{DistrictFestivalDates}}","A district festival cannot be held on the Saturday of the State Marching Band Championships, or from Wednesday to Saturday of the Wisconsin State Music Conference, or on other dates set by WSMA.","A district festival with Class A entries must be held at least three weeks before the State Solo & Ensemble Festival assigned to the schools taking part.","WSMA assigns each school to a State Solo & Ensemble Festival site. A school's assigned state site is listed on the WSMA website.","State Solo & Ensemble Festivals are held on {{StateFestivalDates}}.","WSMA schedules all state festival events and assigns all adjudicators. Schedules are posted on the WSMA website.","A student who qualifies in more than ten events can enter at most ten events per state festival date. The student may move solo events to another state festival date.","A soloist or ensemble that qualifies may perform a different selection at state than at district. All event header rules in the Festival Music List must still be followed.","Regular Ensemble members must be the same at district and state. Substitutions are not allowed.","A Special Ensemble may perform at the State Festival for a rating even if not all members are present. Substitutions are not allowed.","A student may not perform the same solo music at WSMA District Festivals in grades 6 to 12."]},{"Key":"fees","Title":"Fees","Items":["Fees are paid per entry, with the registration, through the WSMA online festival registration system.","District fees per entry: {{DistrictRegistrationFees}}.","State fees per entry: {{StateRegistrationFees}}.","Late registration: {{LateRegistrationFee}}. A late state site transfer or split-site request after November 15: {{StateTransferFee}}.","A home-schooled student or a student from a non-member school also pays the annual fee: {{NonMemberFee}}.","School membership dues (paid by the school): {{MembershipDues}}","WSMA memberships run from July 1 to June 30."]},{"Key":"dates","Title":"Dates","Items":["District festival dates: {{DistrictFestivalDates}}","State festival dates: {{StateFestivalDates}}.","All district festival dates must reach WSMA by {{DistrictDatesDeadline}}."]},{"Key":"venue","Title":"Venue","Items":["WSMA assigns each school to a district festival site and to a state festival site. A school's state site is listed on the WSMA website.","The WSMA office address is {{WsmaAddress}}."]},{"Key":"points","Title":"Ratings (no places)","Items":["There are no places or ranks. Every entry gets a Rating, shown by Roman numerals, based on how well it matches a standard. Many entries can earn the same rating.","Starred I (*I): an exceptional performance in all areas. This rating applies to Class A Solo & Ensemble events only.","I: a consistently outstanding performance in all areas.","II: a very good performance with only minor issues.","III: a performance that shows accomplishment but is weak in several qualities.","IV: a performance with numerous weaknesses.","V: a performance that shows a need for continued effort in fundamentals. This rating applies only to District Solo & Ensemble Festivals.","No Rating (NR): a rule violation on the day of the festival makes the event ineligible to receive a rating.","Comment Only (CO): a teacher can register any festival event as Comment Only. The adjudicator gives written and verbal feedback but no rating, and the rating is reported as CO.","The adjudicator scores the performance on a rubric form with sub-captions, adds the sub-caption values, and the total score decides the final rating. A lower total is a better rating.","The rubric form has 5 criteria or 6 criteria. The class entered sets what is expected within each criterion. The rating bands for each class are in the class rules.","Ratings are available at every festival. District festivals may post ratings online. All State Solo & Ensemble ratings are posted online."]},{"Key":"prizes","Title":"Prizes and Recognition","Items":["Exemplary Performance Recognition Project: it recognises truly outstanding student performances at the State Solo & Ensemble Festival.","To be eligible, the performance must be a Class A State solo or ensemble, must receive a 1 in all rubric categories, and must go above and beyond what the rubric expects.","WSMA sets the number of nominations and awards for each performance site in proportion to the number of events at that site. Adjudicators do not have to use all the nominations or awards available.","Step 1: right after the performance, the adjudicator decides the nominations and marks them on the adjudicator schedule. The adjudicator does not tell the performers about a nomination. The adjudicator schedule is for internal use only.","Step 2: at the end of the festival, the adjudicator reviews all nominations, chooses up to the allowed number of events for the award and marks them on the adjudicator schedule.","Nominations are shown on the WSMA website by the end of business on the Tuesday after the festival.","Award winners are also shown on the WSMA website by that time, receive an Exemplary Performance Recognition Certificate and a personal congratulatory letter mailed to the school, and are named in a press release sent to local news media.","Certificates and letters are mailed in the second week of May, after all State Solo & Ensemble Festivals end.","Each student in an awarded solo, duet, trio, quartet or quintet receives their own certificate with student name, school and event name. An event of 6 or more performers receives one certificate with the school name and event name. More digital certificates can be sent by email to the WSMA office on request."]},{"Key":"teams","Title":"Entry Limits","Items":["A student can enter at most ten Solo & Ensemble events at one district festival.","At a district festival, a student can enter each solo event only once in the school year.","A student can enter up to three vocal solos: one from events 1111, 1121, 1211, 1221 or 1231, one from events 1311, 1321 or 1331, and one from event 6211.","To perform in music theater events 1311, 1321, 1331 or 1382, a student must perform a Class A solo from events 1111, 1121, 1211, 1221 or 1231, or a Class A duet from events 1182, 1282 or 1882, at a WSMA District Festival.","A student can enter the same Regular or Special Ensemble event more than once if the selection and the ensemble members are different for each entry.","A student who qualifies for state can enter at most ten events per state festival date."]},{"Key":"conduct","Title":"Conduct and Festival Rules","Items":["A student musician must follow their school's code of conduct all year round at all WSMA-sanctioned events. School leaders, with the WSMA Executive Director, decide the consequences of breaking the code of conduct, including unacceptable conduct that goes against the ideals, principles and standards of the school and WSMA.","During a festival, no teacher or other person may approach students, parents or staff on school premises to ask them to subscribe or give money to, join, work for or take part in any organization or event.","All WSMA Solo & Ensemble events are open to the public. It is not appropriate to clear any room before or during a performance.","Do not record the adjudicator's critique or clinic, for any reason. Adjudicators cannot give permission to record. The parent or teacher decides whether to record the student's own performance, in line with federal copyright law.","A second performance is not allowed under any circumstances. After a student has performed and received an official rating, the performance is final. There is no second performance and no re-rating.","A rule violation on the day of the festival gives the performance a result of No Rating (NR). No event is prevented from performing because of a rule violation.","If a rule problem is found before the performance, the student is told so it can be fixed. If it cannot be fixed, the student may perform for No Rating, or choose to perform at a later festival.","Any performance with music that is not on the current Festival Music List and has no WSMA permission approval letter attached to the original score is evaluated as No Rating."]},{"Key":"judging","Title":"Judging","Items":["All events are adjudicated. Adjudicators give written and verbal feedback as well as ratings.","Adjudicators write comments on the back of the rubric form, and talk to the student right after the performance.","Adjudicators make every effort to hear the full performance. If time is short, the adjudicator may shorten the verbal comments to listen longer and move detailed feedback to the written critique.","Every performance must begin with a student introduction. The introduction does not count in the rating.","An adjudicator does not play a student's instrument."]},{"Key":"other","Title":"Classes Not in This Template","Items":["Concert Festivals (Class A, B, C and M: concert band, orchestra and choir groups) have their own rules and fees in the same Rules document. They are not part of this template.","Clinic Experience for concert groups gives comments only, with no ratings and no concert plaques. It is not part of this template.","Class M (middle school, up to and including 9th grade) applies only to concert group events.","Other WSMA activities (State Honors Music Project, State Marching Band, Student Composition Project) are not part of this template."]}],"Documents":[{"Title":"2026-2027 WSMA Festival Rules","Url":"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1KNfzqPMasmooPUWGcrkHYuk7BOwUNdqkrS-y4rfQL8g/edit?usp=sharing"},{"Title":"2026-2027 WSMA Adjudicator Handbook","Url":"https://docs.google.com/document/d/16lGnRjj_k1KujY5aV6eBfJCkneK9cIoYjmEld-mv_AA/edit?usp=sharing"},{"Title":"WSMA Dues and Fees 2026-2027","Url":"https://wsmamusic.org/files/2023/03/WSMA_Dues_Fees_26-27.pdf"},{"Title":"Festival Rules page","Url":"https://wsmamusic.org/festival-resources/rules/"},{"Title":"Permissions, alternate editions and music transfers","Url":"https://wsmamusic.org/permissions"}]}`),a=[{ParticipantType:"Individual",Source:"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules, District Solo Performance Requirements; WSMA 2026-2027 Adjudicator Handbook",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Each soloist introduces the performance: performer and accompanist names, school name, title of the selection (and movements if needed), composer, and a significant aspect of the selection (style, form, harmony, melody, rhythm or technique).","Memorizing the music is optional, except in music theater events 1311, 1321 and 1331.","Accompaniment is strongly encouraged but not required, except in music theater events 1311, 1321 and 1331, where it is required.","A pre-recorded accompaniment, or another instrument in place of the piano, is allowed in all solo events, including music theater."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Each soloist introduces the performance: performer and accompanist names, school name, title of the selection (and movements if needed), composer, and a significant aspect of the selection (style, form, harmony, melody, rhythm or technique).
- Memorizing the music is optional, except in music theater events 1311, 1321 and 1331.
- Accompaniment is strongly encouraged but not required, except in music theater events 1311, 1321 and 1331, where it is required.
- A pre-recorded accompaniment, or another instrument in place of the piano, is allowed in all solo events, including music theater.`},{ParticipantType:"Team",Source:"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules, District Ensemble Performance Requirements; WSMA 2026-2027 Adjudicator Handbook",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["A student member of the ensemble introduces the performance: school name, title of the selection (and movements if needed), composer, and a significant aspect of the selection (style, form, harmony, melody, rhythm or technique). The introduction does not need to be memorized.","An ensemble that stays substantially the same next year must play a different selection than at the previous district festival.","Students in an ensemble may come from several schools if a co-op ensemble form is sent to WSMA and all the conditions in Eligibility are met.","Accompaniment, when it applies, is strongly encouraged but not required. Music theater duet 1382 requires accompaniment."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- A student member of the ensemble introduces the performance: school name, title of the selection (and movements if needed), composer, and a significant aspect of the selection (style, form, harmony, melody, rhythm or technique). The introduction does not need to be memorized.
- An ensemble that stays substantially the same next year must play a different selection than at the previous district festival.
- Students in an ensemble may come from several schools if a co-op ensemble form is sent to WSMA and all the conditions in Eligibility are met.
- Accompaniment, when it applies, is strongly encouraged but not required. Music theater duet 1382 requires accompaniment.`}],n=[{AgeGroup:"Cls C 1-2y exp",Source:"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules, Classes Defined and Ratings; WSMA 2026-2027 Adjudicator Handbook, Rating Information and Reminders",Sections:{About:["Class C is for students with one or two years of musical experience.","Grade 6 students are strongly recommended to start in Class C."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["In a Class C vocal event, when English and another language are printed in the edition, the performers may sing either language.","There is no limit on transposition in Class C vocal events."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:["Rating bands on a 5-criteria form (lower is better): I 5-11, II 12-22, III 23-33, IV 34-44, V 45-50.","Rating bands on a 6-criteria form: I 6-12, II 13-24, III 25-36, IV 37-48, V 49-60.","There is no starred I in Class C."],Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Class C is for students with one or two years of musical experience.
- Grade 6 students are strongly recommended to start in Class C.

## Rules
- In a Class C vocal event, when English and another language are printed in the edition, the performers may sing either language.
- There is no limit on transposition in Class C vocal events.

## Judging
- Rating bands on a 5-criteria form (lower is better): I 5-11, II 12-22, III 23-33, IV 34-44, V 45-50.
- Rating bands on a 6-criteria form: I 6-12, II 13-24, III 25-36, IV 37-48, V 49-60.
- There is no starred I in Class C.`},{AgeGroup:"Cls B 3-4y exp",Source:"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules, Classes Defined and Ratings; WSMA 2026-2027 Adjudicator Handbook, Rating Information",Sections:{About:["Class B is for students with three or four years of musical experience."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["In a Class B vocal event, when English and another language are printed in the edition, the performers may sing either language."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:["Rating bands on a 5-criteria form (lower is better): I 5-11, II 12-22, III 23-33, IV 34-44, V 45-50.","Rating bands on a 6-criteria form: I 6-12, II 13-24, III 25-36, IV 37-48, V 49-60.","There is no starred I in Class B."],Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Class B is for students with three or four years of musical experience.

## Rules
- In a Class B vocal event, when English and another language are printed in the edition, the performers may sing either language.

## Judging
- Rating bands on a 5-criteria form (lower is better): I 5-11, II 12-22, III 23-33, IV 34-44, V 45-50.
- Rating bands on a 6-criteria form: I 6-12, II 13-24, III 25-36, IV 37-48, V 49-60.
- There is no starred I in Class B.`},{AgeGroup:"Cls A advanced",Source:"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules, Classes Defined, Ratings, District and State Performance Requirements; WSMA 2026-2027 Adjudicator Handbook, Rating Information",Sections:{About:["Class A is for advanced high school musical experience."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["In a Class A vocal solo or vocal ensemble, the selection must be performed in the original language in which it is written. If the original language is not printed in the edition, performers may use either the published or the original language.","In the introduction of a Class A vocal solo or ensemble, also say the language in which the selection will be performed.","Event 6211 (vocal jazz improvisation solo) is a Class A event only."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:["District rating bands on a 5-criteria form (lower is better): starred I 5-8, I 9-11, II 12-22, III 23-33, IV 34-44, V 45-50.","District rating bands on a 6-criteria form: starred I 6-9, I 10-12, II 13-24, III 25-36, IV 37-48, V 49-60.","State rating bands on a 5-criteria form: I 5-7, II 8-17, III 18-25, IV 26-30.","State rating bands on a 6-criteria form: I 6-8, II 9-20, III 21-32, IV 33-36.","Rating V applies only to district festivals."],Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Class A is for advanced high school musical experience.

## Rules
- In a Class A vocal solo or vocal ensemble, the selection must be performed in the original language in which it is written. If the original language is not printed in the edition, performers may use either the published or the original language.
- In the introduction of a Class A vocal solo or ensemble, also say the language in which the selection will be performed.
- Event 6211 (vocal jazz improvisation solo) is a Class A event only.

## Judging
- District rating bands on a 5-criteria form (lower is better): starred I 5-8, I 9-11, II 12-22, III 23-33, IV 34-44, V 45-50.
- District rating bands on a 6-criteria form: starred I 6-9, I 10-12, II 13-24, III 25-36, IV 37-48, V 49-60.
- State rating bands on a 5-criteria form: I 5-7, II 8-17, III 18-25, IV 26-30.
- State rating bands on a 6-criteria form: I 6-8, II 9-20, III 21-32, IV 33-36.
- Rating V applies only to district festivals.`}],i=JSON.parse(`[{"Title":"Solo","Source":"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules, District Solo Performance Requirements; WSMA 2026-2027 Adjudicator Handbook","Sections":{"About":["Solo events are vocal, string, woodwind, brass, percussion, piano, guitar and handbell."],"TimeAndFormat":["Vocal solos are sung in full, so they fit the time slot without cuts."],"Rules":["Music theater events 1311, 1321 and 1331: the introduction gives performer and accompanist names, school name, and the title and composer of the selection. Instead of describing a significant aspect of the piece, the student briefly sets the scene by describing the character's circumstances and the purpose of the song.","Music theater events 1311, 1321 and 1331 require memorization and accompaniment.","Events 1231 and 1331 (changing or transitioning voice) have no limit on transposition.","Jazz improvisation solo events (such as 6211 and 6311): use pre-recorded or live accompaniment that includes a rhythm section covering rhythmic, harmonic and melodic elements (any mix of piano, bass, drum set and guitar). The student brings their own playback device.","Event 6211 (vocal jazz improvisation solo): key, tempo and style are the performer's choice, the student can sing the high or low voice edition, and a student or adult may play the first pitch. The default form is to sing the head, scat or improvise the first half of the repeat of the head, and sing the rest of the song with melodic or rhythmic changes, with at least 16 bars of improvisation. A song's own instructions may give an exception.","Event 8211 (guitar solo): limited permission is allowed for ukulele solos."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Solo events are vocal, string, woodwind, brass, percussion, piano, guitar and handbell.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Vocal solos are sung in full, so they fit the time slot without cuts.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Music theater events 1311, 1321 and 1331: the introduction gives performer and accompanist names, school name, and the title and composer of the selection. Instead of describing a significant aspect of the piece, the student briefly sets the scene by describing the character's circumstances and the purpose of the song.\\n- Music theater events 1311, 1321 and 1331 require memorization and accompaniment.\\n- Events 1231 and 1331 (changing or transitioning voice) have no limit on transposition.\\n- Jazz improvisation solo events (such as 6211 and 6311): use pre-recorded or live accompaniment that includes a rhythm section covering rhythmic, harmonic and melodic elements (any mix of piano, bass, drum set and guitar). The student brings their own playback device.\\n- Event 6211 (vocal jazz improvisation solo): key, tempo and style are the performer's choice, the student can sing the high or low voice edition, and a student or adult may play the first pitch. The default form is to sing the head, scat or improvise the first half of the repeat of the head, and sing the rest of the song with melodic or rhythmic changes, with at least 16 bars of improvisation. A song's own instructions may give an exception.\\n- Event 8211 (guitar solo): limited permission is allowed for ukulele solos."},{"Title":"Regular Ensemble","Source":"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules, District Ensemble Performance Requirements; WSMA 2026-2027 Adjudicator Handbook, Regular vs Special Ensembles","Sections":{"About":["A Regular Ensemble has a small, set number of performers (duets, trios, quartets and so on) in vocal, string, woodwind, brass, percussion, piano, guitar or handbell groups."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Conducting is not allowed in a Regular Ensemble.","A Regular Ensemble that is missing a member receives No Rating at both district and state.","A pre-recorded accompaniment is allowed for Regular Ensembles, including music theater duet 1382. Another accompaniment instrument may replace the piano.","Music theater duet 1382: memorization and accompaniment are required. The introduction gives performer names, accompanist name, school name, and the title and composer of the selection, and the student briefly sets the scene by describing the character's circumstances and the purpose of the song. The selection may be moved up or down by as much as a minor third to suit the voices.","Event 9116 (miscellaneous two-part mixed ensemble for 3 to 6 performers, Class C only): music may be moved to it from any Class C duet list."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A Regular Ensemble has a small, set number of performers (duets, trios, quartets and so on) in vocal, string, woodwind, brass, percussion, piano, guitar or handbell groups.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Conducting is not allowed in a Regular Ensemble.\\n- A Regular Ensemble that is missing a member receives No Rating at both district and state.\\n- A pre-recorded accompaniment is allowed for Regular Ensembles, including music theater duet 1382. Another accompaniment instrument may replace the piano.\\n- Music theater duet 1382: memorization and accompaniment are required. The introduction gives performer names, accompanist name, school name, and the title and composer of the selection, and the student briefly sets the scene by describing the character's circumstances and the purpose of the song. The selection may be moved up or down by as much as a minor third to suit the voices.\\n- Event 9116 (miscellaneous two-part mixed ensemble for 3 to 6 performers, Class C only): music may be moved to it from any Class C duet list."},{"Title":"Special Ensemble","Source":"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules, District Ensemble Performance Requirements; WSMA 2026-2027 Adjudicator Handbook, Regular vs Special Ensembles","Sections":{"About":["A Special Ensemble is a larger group such as a flute choir or show choir.","Special Ensemble events include: miscellaneous ensemble, jazz ensemble, jazz combo, madrigal, vocal jazz, show choir, contemporary a cappella, ethnic heritage ensemble, string, woodwind and brass choir or ensemble, percussion ensemble, guitar ensemble and handbell choir."],"TimeAndFormat":["These instrumental Special Ensembles set up and perform one piece within 12 minutes: 2119 Violin Choir, 2219 Viola Choir, 2319 Cello Choir, 2419 String Bass Choir, 2859 String Choir, 2959 Misc. String Ensemble, 3839 Flute Choir, 3849 Clarinet Choir, 3859 Saxophone Choir, 3869 Recorder Ensemble, 3878 Woodwind Ensemble, 4858 Brass Ensemble, 4859 Brass Choir, 4868 Brass & Percussion Ensemble, 5839 Keyboard Mallet Ensemble, 5859 Steel Drum Band, 5918 Misc. Percussion Ensemble, 8218 Guitar Ensemble, 9119 Misc. Special Ensemble, and the ethnic, folk and heritage ensembles 9218 (mostly winds), 9228 (mostly percussion), 9238 (mostly strings) and 9248 (mostly vocal).","Events 9218, 9228, 9238 and 9248 must perform for at least 5 minutes, or another selection must be added.","These vocal Special Ensembles set up and perform one piece within 10 minutes: 1519 SSA/A Madrigal Ensemble, 1529 Madrigal Ensemble and 1918 Misc. Vocal Ensemble.","Handbell Choir (8119) sets up and performs one piece within 16 minutes.","These Special Ensembles set up and perform two contrasting style or tempo pieces within 20 minutes: 6118 SSA/A Show Choir, 6119 SSA/A Show Choir Open Class, 6128 TTB/B Show Choir, 6138 Mixed Show Choir, 6139 Mixed Show Choir Open Class, 6228 SSA/A Vocal Jazz Ensemble, 6229 SSA/A Vocal Jazz Ensemble Open Class, 6238 Mixed Vocal Jazz Ensemble, 6239 Mixed Vocal Jazz Ensemble Open Class, 6258 SSA/A Contemporary A Cappella, 6268 TTB/B Contemporary A Cappella, 6278 Mixed Contemporary A Cappella, 6329 Alternative String Ensemble, 6458 Jazz Ensemble, 6459 Jazz Ensemble Open Class, 6468 Jazz Combo and 6478 Creative Jazz Combo.","One piece for events 6458, 6459, 6468 and 6478 must be a swing or shuffle tune (a shuffle is a type of swing)."],"Rules":["A Special Ensemble may be conducted by a student or an adult.","Exceptions: Misc. Vocal (1918) and the Madrigals (1519 and 1529) may be directed only by a student who performs inside the ensemble.","Steel Drum Band (5859) may be directed only by an adult, or by a student who keeps time from inside the ensemble on traditional steel drum instruments (not a drum set).","Jazz Combos (6468 and 6478) may not be conducted by an adult.","Pre-recorded accompaniments are not allowed for Special Ensembles unless the event header in the Festival Music List says so. Show choir and vocal jazz ensembles may use pre-recorded instrumental tracks if there are no background vocal lines on the track.","All instrumental jazz ensembles: one selection must be a swing or shuffle."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A Special Ensemble is a larger group such as a flute choir or show choir.\\n- Special Ensemble events include: miscellaneous ensemble, jazz ensemble, jazz combo, madrigal, vocal jazz, show choir, contemporary a cappella, ethnic heritage ensemble, string, woodwind and brass choir or ensemble, percussion ensemble, guitar ensemble and handbell choir.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- These instrumental Special Ensembles set up and perform one piece within 12 minutes: 2119 Violin Choir, 2219 Viola Choir, 2319 Cello Choir, 2419 String Bass Choir, 2859 String Choir, 2959 Misc. String Ensemble, 3839 Flute Choir, 3849 Clarinet Choir, 3859 Saxophone Choir, 3869 Recorder Ensemble, 3878 Woodwind Ensemble, 4858 Brass Ensemble, 4859 Brass Choir, 4868 Brass & Percussion Ensemble, 5839 Keyboard Mallet Ensemble, 5859 Steel Drum Band, 5918 Misc. Percussion Ensemble, 8218 Guitar Ensemble, 9119 Misc. Special Ensemble, and the ethnic, folk and heritage ensembles 9218 (mostly winds), 9228 (mostly percussion), 9238 (mostly strings) and 9248 (mostly vocal).\\n- Events 9218, 9228, 9238 and 9248 must perform for at least 5 minutes, or another selection must be added.\\n- These vocal Special Ensembles set up and perform one piece within 10 minutes: 1519 SSA/A Madrigal Ensemble, 1529 Madrigal Ensemble and 1918 Misc. Vocal Ensemble.\\n- Handbell Choir (8119) sets up and performs one piece within 16 minutes.\\n- These Special Ensembles set up and perform two contrasting style or tempo pieces within 20 minutes: 6118 SSA/A Show Choir, 6119 SSA/A Show Choir Open Class, 6128 TTB/B Show Choir, 6138 Mixed Show Choir, 6139 Mixed Show Choir Open Class, 6228 SSA/A Vocal Jazz Ensemble, 6229 SSA/A Vocal Jazz Ensemble Open Class, 6238 Mixed Vocal Jazz Ensemble, 6239 Mixed Vocal Jazz Ensemble Open Class, 6258 SSA/A Contemporary A Cappella, 6268 TTB/B Contemporary A Cappella, 6278 Mixed Contemporary A Cappella, 6329 Alternative String Ensemble, 6458 Jazz Ensemble, 6459 Jazz Ensemble Open Class, 6468 Jazz Combo and 6478 Creative Jazz Combo.\\n- One piece for events 6458, 6459, 6468 and 6478 must be a swing or shuffle tune (a shuffle is a type of swing).\\n\\n## Rules\\n- A Special Ensemble may be conducted by a student or an adult.\\n- Exceptions: Misc. Vocal (1918) and the Madrigals (1519 and 1529) may be directed only by a student who performs inside the ensemble.\\n- Steel Drum Band (5859) may be directed only by an adult, or by a student who keeps time from inside the ensemble on traditional steel drum instruments (not a drum set).\\n- Jazz Combos (6468 and 6478) may not be conducted by an adult.\\n- Pre-recorded accompaniments are not allowed for Special Ensembles unless the event header in the Festival Music List says so. Show choir and vocal jazz ensembles may use pre-recorded instrumental tracks if there are no background vocal lines on the track.\\n- All instrumental jazz ensembles: one selection must be a swing or shuffle."}]`),o=[],r=[{Key:"DistrictFestivalDates",Kind:"text",Label:"District Solo & Ensemble Festival dates",Value:"Set by the participating schools in each district.",Source:"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules, District Festival Requirements"},{Key:"DistrictDatesDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"Last date for a district to send its festival dates to WSMA",Value:"October 1 of the current school year",Source:"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules, District Festival Requirements"},{Key:"DistrictSiteChangeDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"Last date to ask for a change of district festival site",Value:"September 15 of the current school year",Source:"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules, District Festival Requirements"},{Key:"DistrictRegistrationDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"District registration deadline",Value:"At least four weeks before the district festival (the fee schedule says a minimum of 20 business days)",Source:"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules, District Festival Requirements; WSMA Dues and Fees 2026-2027"},{Key:"StateFestivalDates",Kind:"date",Label:"State Solo & Ensemble Festival dates",Value:"The last Saturday of April and the first Saturday of May, every year",Source:"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules, State Festival Requirements"},{Key:"StateRegistrationDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"State registration deadline",Value:"At least 15 business days before the assigned State Solo & Ensemble Festival",Source:"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules, State Festival Requirements"},{Key:"StateTransferDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"Last date to ask for a state festival site transfer",Value:"November 15 of the current school year",Source:"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules, State Festival Requirements"},{Key:"DistrictRegistrationFees",Kind:"fee",Label:"District Solo & Ensemble fees per entry",Value:"USD 13.00 for a Class A solo, USD 9.00 for all other solos, USD 16.00 for a Regular Ensemble, USD 23.00 for a Special Ensemble, USD 40.00 for a Jazz Ensemble, Jazz Combo, Show Choir, Vocal Jazz or Alternative Strings ensemble",Source:"WSMA Dues and Fees 2026-2027, Program Fees"},{Key:"StateRegistrationFees",Kind:"fee",Label:"State Solo & Ensemble fees per entry",Value:"USD 20.00 for a Class A solo, USD 22.00 for a Regular Ensemble, USD 29.00 for a Special Ensemble, USD 46.00 for a Jazz Ensemble, Jazz Combo, Show Choir, Vocal Jazz or Alternative Strings ensemble",Source:"WSMA Dues and Fees 2026-2027, Program Fees"},{Key:"LateRegistrationFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Late registration fee",Value:"USD 5.00 per entry, not more than USD 75.00 (district and state)",Source:"WSMA Dues and Fees 2026-2027"},{Key:"StateTransferFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Late state site transfer or split-site request fee",Value:"USD 6.00 per entry (requests received after November 15)",Source:"WSMA Dues and Fees 2026-2027"},{Key:"NonMemberFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Annual fee for home-schooled students and students from non-member schools",Value:"USD 58.00 for access to all WSMA programs and services",Source:"WSMA Dues and Fees 2026-2027"},{Key:"MembershipDues",Kind:"fee",Label:"School membership dues (2026-2027)",Value:"High schools: USD 212.00 (1 to 75 students) up to USD 664.00 (1501 or more students) if paid by October 15, 2026, and USD 50.00 more after that date. Elementary, middle and junior high schools: USD 117.00 (1 to 50 students) up to USD 515.00 (701 or more students) by October 15, 2026, and USD 50.00 more after that date; dues are based on enrollment in grade 6 and above. Elementary schools (K-5): USD 60.00 by October 15, 2026 and USD 110.00 after. Membership runs from July 1 to June 30. A 2% finance charge a month (24% APR) applies to balances unpaid after 45 days.",Source:"WSMA Dues and Fees 2026-2027"},{Key:"WsmaOffice",Kind:"contact",Label:"WSMA office (Festivals Support Team)",Value:{Name:"Wisconsin School Music Association",Role:"Festivals Support Team",Phone:"800-589-9762 or 608-850-3566 (Madison area)",WhatsApp:"",Email:""},Source:"WSMA festival rules page (wsmamusic.org/festival-resources/rules/); WSMA 2026-2027 Adjudicator Handbook"},{Key:"WsmaAddress",Kind:"address",Label:"WSMA office address",Value:{Name:"Wisconsin School Music Association",Address:"1005 Quinn Drive",City:"Waunakee, WI 53597",MapUrl:"",Directions:""},Source:"WSMA 2026-2027 Adjudicator Handbook"},{Key:"OnlineRegistration",Kind:"link",Label:"WSMA online festival registration system and fees",Value:"https://wsmamusic.org/fees/",Source:"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules (registration comments); WSMA Dues and Fees 2026-2027"},{Key:"PermissionsLink",Kind:"link",Label:"Permissions, alternate editions and music transfers",Value:"https://wsmamusic.org/permissions",Source:"WSMA 2026-2027 Festival Rules"}],l=[{Claim:"Every rule, class, rating, entry limit, deadline, permission and state-qualification rule on the page comes from the 2026-2027 WSMA Festival Rules (the current edition; the template cites the 2024-2025 edition).",SourceType:"Primary - the event's own rules document",Source:"2026-2027 WSMA Festival Rules and Information",Url:"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1KNfzqPMasmooPUWGcrkHYuk7BOwUNdqkrS-y4rfQL8g/edit?usp=sharing"},{Claim:"Rating bands (inverted scale, lower total is better), Regular vs Special Ensemble differences, conducting exceptions, missing-member rule and age/grade neutrality come from the 2026-2027 Adjudicator Handbook.",SourceType:"Primary - the event's own handbook",Source:"2026-2027 WSMA Adjudicator Handbook",Url:"https://docs.google.com/document/d/16lGnRjj_k1KujY5aV6eBfJCkneK9cIoYjmEld-mv_AA/edit?usp=sharing"},{Claim:"Entry fees, late fees, non-member fee and membership dues come from the 2026-2027 WSMA Dues and Fees schedule.",SourceType:"Primary - the event's own fee schedule",Source:"WSMA Dues and Fees 2026-2027",Url:"https://wsmamusic.org/files/2023/03/WSMA_Dues_Fees_26-27.pdf"}],d={TemplateKey:e,Year:2026,Note:t,Page:s,ParticipantTypes:a,AgeGroups:n,Competitions:i,CompetitionAgeGroups:o,Variables:r,Evidence:l};export{n as AgeGroups,o as CompetitionAgeGroups,i as Competitions,l as Evidence,t as Note,s as Page,a as ParticipantTypes,e as TemplateKey,r as Variables,c as Year,d as default};
